{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012WL9RZwFnGyZrrBU7RGKLv","type":"tool_result","content":"commit c85b749317bb80d015d15187dd16da4a94c40510\nAuthor: Jakub <neumie@neumie.dev>\nDate:   Tue May 26 16:10:29 2026 +0200\n\n    fix(converge): overseer parser captures multi-line REASON/STEER and value-on-next-line\n    \n    Bug observed in the first --prompt converge run: overseer.log tick=1\n    showed `REASON:` empty and `STEER: none` even though the LLM was asked\n    to produce both as `<one paragraph>` values. Root cause: the parser was\n    line-based — for each `KEY:` marker it captured only the value text on\n    THE SAME LINE. Any LLM that emitted the value on the next line or\n    spanned a paragraph across multiple lines (which is the natural way to\n    write a paragraph in markdown-ish output) got silently truncated to\n    empty or to the first line of the paragraph.\n    \n    The bug effect was severe: with REASON empty and STEER none in every\n    overseer tick, the next worker round received no guidance from the\n    previous round's audit. Convergence became \"each round picks at\n    random whatever subset of findings it feels like applying\", which is\n    what we observed (round 1 surveyed and stalled; round 2 picked 3-of-8\n    on its own).\n    \n    Fix: rewrite the parser as a state-machine line walker. When a KEY:\n    prefix is seen, switch to that field and capture the value tail (could\n    be empty if value is on the next line). All subsequent non-key lines\n    get appended to the currently-open field until the next KEY: marker\n    switches state. Three new behaviors supported:\n      - Multi-line REASON / STEER paragraphs (the common case)\n      - Value on line after KEY: (`REASON:\\nthe reason text`)\n      - Chatty VERDICT: token-then-prose-then-REASON; VERDICT is reduced","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VBXnEJr4hBEm1954BzWA3K","session_id":"7f32d037-1d78-4726-b16f-75668d38d257","uuid":"1cca9eda-3cb0-476a-b2c9-ead877991440","timestamp":"2026-05-26T14:26:41.995Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Find architectural friction in almanac"}
