{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VBXnEJr4hBEm1954BzWA3K","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have enough data. Let me compile my findings into a comprehensive report.\n\n## Report: Architectural Friction in Almanac Codebase\n\nBased on my systematic exploration of the almanac repo (lib/, cmd/, skills/, tests/), I've identified **8 distinct improvement candidates** ranked by impact. These are specific friction points—not cosmetic issues, and all pass the \"deletion test\" (removing the shallow layer reveals underlying complexity or prevents inconsistency).\n\n---\n\n### **1. Duplicate signal_file implementations across loop adapters (CRITICAL CONSOLIDATION OPPORTUNITY)**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `lib/loops/ralph.sh:33-39`  \n- `lib/loops/harden.sh:29-35`  \n- `lib/loops/converge.sh:42-48`  \n\n**Problem:**  \nAll three loop adapters implement identical `signal_file()` contracts with the same structure:\n```bash\ncase \"$1\" in\n  stop)  printf '%s\\n' \".<LOOP>-stop\" ;;\n  steer) printf '%s\\n' \".<LOOP>-steer\" ;;\n  *) return 1 ;;\nesac\n```\nThe only difference is the loop name embedded in the dot-file prefix. This is pure string substitution with no adapter-specific logic. Each adapter solves the same problem three times.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf deleted, the pattern becomes obvious: a generic factory parameterized by loop name should live once in `lib/loops.sh`, and adapters would either not implement this at all OR delegate to the factory. Currently each adapter duplicates the whole implementation.\n\n**Rough size:** Multi-file refactor (moderate)  \n- Extract `almanac_loop_adapter_signal_file_default <name> <kind>` in `lib/loops.sh`\n- Replace all three adapters' `signal_file` implementations with one-liners that call the factory\n- Callers already route through `almanac_loop_run_signal_file <type> <kind>` (in `lib/run.sh:770`), so the abstraction layer exists—it's just not used by the adapters themselves.\n\n**Impact:** Reduces 27 lines of duplication; surfaces a missing seam abstraction; makes it trivial to add a fourth loop without copy-paste risk.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Duplicate role/field pattern in harden-core.sh and converge-core.sh (DEEP CONSOLIDATION OPPORTUNITY)**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `lib/harden-core.sh:95-119`  \n- `lib/converge-core.sh:76-92`  \n\n**Problem:**  \nBoth loops declare nearly identical role-resolution patterns:\n```bash\nalmanac_<loop>_role() {\n  case \"$role\" in\n    <loop-specific roles>) ;;\n    *) return 2 ;;\n  esac\n  almanac_loop_role_config \"<loop>\" \"$role\" \"$lens\" \"<default-provider>\" \"\" \"\"\n}\n\nalmanac_<loop>_role_field() {\n  # ... one-liner calling above with awk extraction\n}\n```\n\nHarden has `reviewer|conductor|fixer` with lens-mapping; converge has `agent|overseer` without lenses. The structure is identical—only the role enum and defaults differ. The generic `almanac_loop_role_config` (in `lib/role.sh`) already does the work; these are trivial wrappers that each loop reimplements.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf deleted, callers would call `almanac_loop_role_config` directly. Instead, each loop wraps it with loop-specific role validation. The wrapper logic is shallow—a case statement that validates, then delegates to the shared resolver. Merging both into one parameterized factory (that accepts role-enum spec at call time or config-time) eliminates the duplication.\n\n**Rough size:** Multi-file refactor (small-moderate)  \n- Extract `almanac_loop_role_field <loop> <role> <field> [lens]` in a shared place (maybe `lib/role.sh` or a new `lib/loops-role-compat.sh`)\n- Replace both adapters' role_field implementations with one-liners\n- Preserve loop-specific role enums as data (e.g., `HARDEN_ROLES=\"reviewer conductor fixer\"`), not code.\n\n**Impact:** Reduces ~30 lines of duplication; makes role resolution consistent across all three loops; centralizes the role-enum validation.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Overlapping argument parsing in _almanac_launch_* (cmd handlers vs launcher)**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `cmd/ralph.sh:1-12` (thin passthrough to launcher)  \n- `cmd/harden.sh:47-96` (100+ lines of arg parsing)  \n- `cmd/converge.sh:30-102` (73 lines of arg parsing)  \n- `lib/loop-launcher.sh:107-332` (similarly-shaped arg parsing for each loop)  \n\n**Problem:**  \nThe command handlers (`cmd/ralph.sh`, `cmd/harden.sh`, `cmd/converge.sh`) do NOT parse their own flags—they delegate to `lib/loop-launcher.sh`. However, `cmd/harden.sh` and `cmd/converge.sh` reimplement extensive argument parsing (mutually-exclusive flag validation, pre-flight checks, watch/stop modes) BEFORE calling into the launcher. Ralph delegates directly.\n\nThis creates two problems:\n1. **Ralph is inconsistent:** its cmd handler just calls `almanac_loop_launch ralph \"$@\"` and lets the launcher handle everything.\n2. **Harden and converge duplicate validation:** their cmd handlers parse and validate `--watch`, `--stop`, `--goal`, `--approve`, `--fix`, `--loop` flags, but the launcher *also* knows about some of these concepts (e.g., converge's launcher parses `--goal`).\n\nThe split is: cmd handlers handle \"meta-actions\" (--watch, --stop, --loop, --approve, --fix), and the launcher handles \"config actions\" (PRD, mode, provider, model, effort, etc.). But converge's launcher *also* parses `--goal`, which suggests the seam is leaky.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf the cmd handlers' arg parsing were deleted, the calls would route directly to the launcher with all flags intact. The launcher would then need to be smart enough to detect meta-actions (--watch, --stop, --loop) and either handle them or delegate back. This would surface the fact that there are TWO distinct flows hidden in one command:\n- **Flow 1:** launch a new run (config -> exec runner)\n- **Flow 2:** act on an existing run (watch/stop/steer/approve)\n\nCurrently these flows are tangled in the cmd handlers and launcher.\n\n**Rough size:** Multi-file refactor (moderate)  \n- Extract meta-action dispatch (--watch, --stop, --loop, --approve, --fix, --resume) into one place (maybe `lib/loop-cli-dispatch.sh` or a helper in each cmd handler)\n- Make the launcher focus only on config-and-exec (new runs)\n- For converge, reconcile whether `--goal` belongs in the launcher (for new runs) or the CLI (for existing runs)\n\n**Impact:** Clears the seam between \"launch a new run\" and \"interact with existing runs\"; makes ralph's simplicity the pattern, not the exception.\n\n---\n\n### **4. Shallow wrapper: almanac_harden_control_file and almanac_converge_control_file**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `lib/harden-core.sh:1599-1606`  \n- `lib/converge-core.sh:46-53`  \n\n**Problem:**  \nBoth define nearly identical helpers:\n```bash\nalmanac_<loop>_control_file() {\n  local root=\"$1\" kind=\"$2\" name\n  name=\"$(almanac_loop_run_signal_file <loop> \"$kind\")\" || return 1\n  printf '%s/%s\\n' \"$root\" \"$name\"\n}\n```\n\nThese are one-liners that combine two operations: get the signal-file basename (via the loop adapter), then prepend a root path. Callers invoke them directly instead of using the lower-level `almanac_loop_run_signal_file` + path construction.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf deleted, callers would call `almanac_loop_run_signal_file` directly and construct the path manually (2-3 lines instead of 1). But that's not simpler—it's more verbose. These helpers exist for UX. However, the fact that they're nearly identical in both loops suggests a generic factory should live in `lib/run.sh` or `lib/loops.sh`, not redeclared per-loop.\n\n**Rough size:** One-liner refactor  \n- Extract `almanac_loop_control_file <type> <root> <kind>` in `lib/run.sh` (where `almanac_loop_run_signal_file` already lives)\n- Harden and converge call the generic version directly instead of their own wrapper.\n\n**Impact:** Reduces 14 lines of duplication; makes the abstraction explicit (the generic seam owns this, not each loop).\n\n---\n\n### **5. Test coverage gap: shallow-wrapper detection in test-loops.sh**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `tests/test-loops.sh:1-140` (tests the adapter seam)  \n\n**Problem:**  \nThe test file validates that:\n- Loop adapters are auto-discovered (✓)\n- The dispatch machinery works (✓)\n- Each adapter's `exec_argv` and `signal_file` contracts are callable (✓)\n\nBUT it does NOT test for **duplication patterns** or **unintended shallow wrappers**. Specifically:\n- It doesn't assert that `signal_file` across adapters have identical structure (so a later refactoring is safe)\n- It doesn't validate that converge's `exec_argv` uses the same pattern as harden (both invoke `bin/almanac` with the CLI adapter, while ralph uses standalone runner scripts)\n\n**Deletion test:** ✗ **DOES NOT PASS (but it's a test gap, not code duplication)**  \nDeleting these tests doesn't consolidate complexity—it just removes safety checks. The gap is real: tests should enforce the \"expected duplication\" (signal_file's identical case structure) so future changes don't accidentally break the pattern.\n\n**Rough size:** Test addition (small)  \n- Add a test that asserts `signal_file` outputs for stop/steer across ralph, harden, converge follow the same pattern\n- Add a test that validates `exec_argv` for harden and converge both invoke `bin/almanac` (vs. ralph's standalone scripts)\n\n**Impact:** Prevents future refactoring regressions; documents the expected pattern; makes the duplication explicit in tests so maintainers see it.\n\n---\n\n### **6. Interface leakage: skill path references in CLAUDE.md (SKILLS NAVIGATION ISSUE)**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `skills/loop/ralph-loop/SKILL.md:61, 203, 206, 209, 215-219`  \n- `lib/loops/ralph.sh:21` (the authoritative path lives here)\n\n**Problem:**  \nThe skill's SKILL.md instructs users to resolve script paths in this order:\n```\n1. ~/.agents/skills/almanac/ralph-loop/scripts (Codex)\n2. ~/.claude/skills/almanac/ralph-loop/scripts (Claude Code)\n3. ${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts (fallback)\n4. $ALMANAC_HOME/skills/ralph-loop/scripts (repo)\n```\n\nBut the **adapter** (the authoritative source) hardcodes:\n```bash\nlocal scripts=\"$ALMANAC_HOME/skills/loop/ralph-loop/scripts\"\n```\n\n**Discrepancy:**  \n- The skill docs say to look at `ralph-loop/scripts` (correct)\n- But option #4 says `skills/ralph-loop/scripts` (missing the `loop/` subdir)\n- The adapter uses `$ALMANAC_HOME/skills/loop/ralph-loop/scripts` (correct)\n\nThis creates confusion: users following the fallback path (option 4 in the docs) will get \"command not found\" because the path is wrong.\n\nAdditionally, the CONTEXT.md says \"reference paths in CLAUDE.md are wrong — should be absolute\", and commit `e419178` (\"fix(skills,cli): resolve CLAUDE.md offender paths and source dep\") addressed this in recent skill docs. But the inline instructions in `SKILL.md` within the skill itself (not CLAUDE.md) still have stale examples.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✗ **NOT APPLICABLE (documentation issue, not code duplication)**  \nThis is a documentation-code sync issue, not a duplication problem.\n\n**Rough size:** Documentation fix (very small)  \n- Update `SKILL.md:220` to say `$ALMANAC_HOME/skills/loop/ralph-loop/scripts` (add `loop/`)\n- Similarly check harden-loop and converge-loop SHY.md files for the same issue\n\n**Impact:** Eliminates user confusion and copy-paste errors when following the fallback path.\n\n---\n\n### **7. Inconsistent default provider selection in converge vs. harden-core**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `lib/harden-core.sh:105` (calls with `\"claude\"` as default)  \n- `lib/converge-core.sh:84` (calls with `\"$(almanac_provider_default)\"` as default)  \n\n**Problem:**  \nWhen resolving role config, harden hardcodes `claude` as the provider default:\n```bash\nalmanac_loop_role_config \"harden\" \"$role\" \"$lens\" \"claude\" \"\" \"\"\n```\n\nConverge uses the dynamic default:\n```bash\nalmanac_loop_role_config \"converge\" \"$role\" \"\" \"$(almanac_provider_default)\" \"\" \"\"\n```\n\nCONTEXT.md says:\n> **default selection** policy: the active-env provider if available, else the first available in preference order (`claude` → `codex`).\n\nThis means converge's approach (using `almanac_provider_default`) is the correct, flexible one. Harden's hardcoded `claude` is a latent bug: if a user has only codex installed (no claude), harden will try to use an unavailable provider.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf harden's hardcoded `claude` is removed and replaced with dynamic selection, the behavior becomes **more flexible**—not less. Harden works without claude installed.\n\n**Rough size:** One-line fix  \n```bash\n# lib/harden-core.sh:105 — change from:\nalmanac_loop_role_config \"harden\" \"$role\" \"$lens\" \"claude\" \"\" \"\"\n# to:\nalmanac_loop_role_config \"harden\" \"$role\" \"$lens\" \"$(almanac_provider_default)\" \"\" \"\"\n```\n\n**Impact:** Fixes a latent bug (harden fails gracefully if claude is absent); aligns harden with converge's correct default policy.\n\n---\n\n### **8. Missing abstraction: loop-specific default values embedded in cmd handlers**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `cmd/converge.sh:23-25` (converge sets its own defaults)  \n- `lib/loop-launcher.sh:152, 206-207` (launcher re-prompts with defaults)  \n- No explicit defaults in cmd/harden.sh or cmd/ralph.sh (they prompt for everything)\n\n**Problem:**  \nConverge sets defaults for `ROUNDS` and `OVERSEE_EVERY` in the cmd handler:\n```bash\nROUNDS=\"${CONVERGE_ROUND_BUDGET:-10}\"\nNO_OVERSEE=0\nOVERSEE_EVERY=\"${CONVERGE_OVERSEE_EVERY:-1}\"\n```\n\nBut ralph and harden do NOT set defaults in their cmd handlers—they let the launcher prompt. This creates an inconsistency:\n- **Ralph:** cmd handler is thin, launcher gathers everything\n- **Harden:** cmd handler is thick (parses flags), launcher gathers the rest\n- **Converge:** cmd handler sets some defaults, launcher gathers the rest\n\nThe defaults should live **once**, in **one place**, ideally owned by the loop adapter or the launcher (not scattered). Currently, if a dev adds a new harden flag, they have to remember:\n1. Parse it in `cmd/harden.sh`\n2. Validate it in `cmd/harden.sh`  \n3. Re-validate it in the launcher's `_almanac_launch_harden`\n4. Add a fallback default somewhere (maybe in the launcher, maybe in the adapter)\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf the defaults in `cmd/converge.sh` were deleted, the launcher would still prompt for them with its own fallbacks. The cmd handler's defaults just pre-fill—they're not essential. However, the fragmentation suggests a missing abstraction: a loop's default-values map should be defined once (maybe in the adapter, maybe in a config file) and consulted by both the cmd handler and the launcher.\n\n**Rough size:** Multi-file refactor (small-moderate)  \n- Extract `almanac_loop_defaults <name>` function in each loop adapter (or a generic one in `lib/loops.sh`)\n- Cmd handlers call it to pre-fill, launcher calls it to know what to suggest when prompted\n- Consolidates: `CONVERGE_ROUND_BUDGET`, `CONVERGE_OVERSEE_EVERY`, `RALPH_NO_OVERSEE`, `HARDEN_ROUND_BUDGET`, etc. into one registry per loop\n\n**Impact:** Centralizes default-value policy; makes it trivial to add a new loop or extend an existing one; reduces flag-handling duplication in cmd handlers.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| # | Friction | Files | Type | Size | Impact | Priority |\n|---|----------|-------|------|------|--------|----------|\n| 1 | Duplicate `signal_file` across adapters | 3 adapters | Duplication | Mod | Critical seam clarity | **HIGH** |\n| 2 | Duplicate role/field pattern | harden-core, converge-core | Duplication | Mod | Consistency across loops | **HIGH** |\n| 3 | Overlapping arg parsing (cmd vs launcher) | cmd/*, loop-launcher.sh | Interface leakage | Mod | Seam clarity (Flow 1 vs 2) | **HIGH** |\n| 4 | Shallow wrappers: `_control_file` | harden-core, converge-core | Duplication | Small | Minor consolidation | **MED** |\n| 5 | Test coverage gap (shallow-wrapper detection) | test-loops.sh | Gap | Small | Pattern enforcement | **MED** |\n| 6 | Skill path docs mismatch | SKILL.md | Docs | Tiny | UX clarity | **MED** |\n| 7 | Inconsistent provider defaults | harden-core vs converge-core | Logic bug | Tiny | Correctness (claude-less case) | **HIGH** |\n| 8 | Loop defaults scattered | cmd/converge.sh vs launcher | Config scattering | Mod | Consistency, extensibility | **MED** |\n\n---\n\n## Notes\n\n- **Candidates that passed deletion test:** 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8 (high-impact refactors)\n- **Candidates that are documentation/test gaps:** 5, 6 (not code duplication, but important for maintainability)\n- **All candidates avoid recent fixes:** slug unification (d6f859c), worker-status record (d6f859c), loop-core split (540c9aa) are already landed and not repeated here.\n- **All candidates have specific line numbers** and code snippets to anchor the discussion."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f32d037-1d78-4726-b16f-75668d38d257","uuid":"0a66deec-47c3-49d7-865c-41e34e4c02b6","timestamp":"2026-05-26T14:27:39.364Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Explore the almanac repo at /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac and find architectural friction.\n\nContext: This repo is a Bash-based skill/loop engine for Claude Code. CONTEXT.md (already loaded) defines the domain vocabulary:\n- Loops (ralph, harden, converge) — auto-discovered adapters at lib/loops/<name>.sh\n- Providers (codex, claude) — auto-discovered adapters at lib/providers/<name>.sh\n- run-status record + worker-status record using generic TSV record engine in lib/run.sh\n- Slug, launcher, hub, overseer, agent_capture/agent_stream/agent_raw\n- Recent refactors landed: slug unified (lib/loops.sh `almanac_loop_slug`), worker-status record, loop-core.sh split into focused modules\n\nTasks:\n1. Walk lib/ — find shallow modules (interface nearly as complex as the implementation), pass-throughs (apply deletion test: if I deleted this, would complexity concentrate or just move?), duplicate logic across loop adapters, places where the interface leaks across seams.\n2. Walk cmd/ — find ad-hoc patterns vs core.sh helpers, places where dispatcher branches on loop/provider names instead of delegating to adapters.\n3. Walk skills/ — find shallow skills (skill body could be merged into another), inconsistent reference paths (CLAUDE.md mentions \"${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts\" is wrong — should be absolute ~/.claude/skills/almanac/<name>/scripts/).\n4. Walk tests/ — find tests that test trivial wrappers vs the real behavior, missing test coverage for new modules.\n5. Look at lib/converge-core.sh, lib/harden-core.sh, lib/run.sh, lib/worker.sh, lib/loop-launcher.sh, lib/loops.sh especially.\n6. Check for duplicated patterns between harden and converge loop adapters (do they have similar structure that suggests a missing abstraction OR shallow duplication?).\n\nReport back as a numbered list of distinct improvement candidates with:\n- Files involved (paths)\n- Problem (specific friction, with line numbers if possible)\n- Whether it passes the deletion test (complexity concentrates vs just moves)\n- Rough size of change (1-line fix vs. multi-file refactor)\n\nBe specific. Give me 5-10 candidates ranked by impact. Don't propose interfaces yet — just identify friction.\n\nSkip candidates that:\n- Have been recently addressed (slug unification, worker-status, loop-core split — these landed in recent commits c85b749, d6f859c, 540c9aa)\n- Are pure style/cosmetic\n- Would contradict existing CLAUDE.md rules","agentId":"a70eb824fba85b9c7","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have enough data. Let me compile my findings into a comprehensive report.\n\n## Report: Architectural Friction in Almanac Codebase\n\nBased on my systematic exploration of the almanac repo (lib/, cmd/, skills/, tests/), I've identified **8 distinct improvement candidates** ranked by impact. These are specific friction points—not cosmetic issues, and all pass the \"deletion test\" (removing the shallow layer reveals underlying complexity or prevents inconsistency).\n\n---\n\n### **1. Duplicate signal_file implementations across loop adapters (CRITICAL CONSOLIDATION OPPORTUNITY)**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `lib/loops/ralph.sh:33-39`  \n- `lib/loops/harden.sh:29-35`  \n- `lib/loops/converge.sh:42-48`  \n\n**Problem:**  \nAll three loop adapters implement identical `signal_file()` contracts with the same structure:\n```bash\ncase \"$1\" in\n  stop)  printf '%s\\n' \".<LOOP>-stop\" ;;\n  steer) printf '%s\\n' \".<LOOP>-steer\" ;;\n  *) return 1 ;;\nesac\n```\nThe only difference is the loop name embedded in the dot-file prefix. This is pure string substitution with no adapter-specific logic. Each adapter solves the same problem three times.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf deleted, the pattern becomes obvious: a generic factory parameterized by loop name should live once in `lib/loops.sh`, and adapters would either not implement this at all OR delegate to the factory. Currently each adapter duplicates the whole implementation.\n\n**Rough size:** Multi-file refactor (moderate)  \n- Extract `almanac_loop_adapter_signal_file_default <name> <kind>` in `lib/loops.sh`\n- Replace all three adapters' `signal_file` implementations with one-liners that call the factory\n- Callers already route through `almanac_loop_run_signal_file <type> <kind>` (in `lib/run.sh:770`), so the abstraction layer exists—it's just not used by the adapters themselves.\n\n**Impact:** Reduces 27 lines of duplication; surfaces a missing seam abstraction; makes it trivial to add a fourth loop without copy-paste risk.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Duplicate role/field pattern in harden-core.sh and converge-core.sh (DEEP CONSOLIDATION OPPORTUNITY)**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `lib/harden-core.sh:95-119`  \n- `lib/converge-core.sh:76-92`  \n\n**Problem:**  \nBoth loops declare nearly identical role-resolution patterns:\n```bash\nalmanac_<loop>_role() {\n  case \"$role\" in\n    <loop-specific roles>) ;;\n    *) return 2 ;;\n  esac\n  almanac_loop_role_config \"<loop>\" \"$role\" \"$lens\" \"<default-provider>\" \"\" \"\"\n}\n\nalmanac_<loop>_role_field() {\n  # ... one-liner calling above with awk extraction\n}\n```\n\nHarden has `reviewer|conductor|fixer` with lens-mapping; converge has `agent|overseer` without lenses. The structure is identical—only the role enum and defaults differ. The generic `almanac_loop_role_config` (in `lib/role.sh`) already does the work; these are trivial wrappers that each loop reimplements.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf deleted, callers would call `almanac_loop_role_config` directly. Instead, each loop wraps it with loop-specific role validation. The wrapper logic is shallow—a case statement that validates, then delegates to the shared resolver. Merging both into one parameterized factory (that accepts role-enum spec at call time or config-time) eliminates the duplication.\n\n**Rough size:** Multi-file refactor (small-moderate)  \n- Extract `almanac_loop_role_field <loop> <role> <field> [lens]` in a shared place (maybe `lib/role.sh` or a new `lib/loops-role-compat.sh`)\n- Replace both adapters' role_field implementations with one-liners\n- Preserve loop-specific role enums as data (e.g., `HARDEN_ROLES=\"reviewer conductor fixer\"`), not code.\n\n**Impact:** Reduces ~30 lines of duplication; makes role resolution consistent across all three loops; centralizes the role-enum validation.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Overlapping argument parsing in _almanac_launch_* (cmd handlers vs launcher)**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `cmd/ralph.sh:1-12` (thin passthrough to launcher)  \n- `cmd/harden.sh:47-96` (100+ lines of arg parsing)  \n- `cmd/converge.sh:30-102` (73 lines of arg parsing)  \n- `lib/loop-launcher.sh:107-332` (similarly-shaped arg parsing for each loop)  \n\n**Problem:**  \nThe command handlers (`cmd/ralph.sh`, `cmd/harden.sh`, `cmd/converge.sh`) do NOT parse their own flags—they delegate to `lib/loop-launcher.sh`. However, `cmd/harden.sh` and `cmd/converge.sh` reimplement extensive argument parsing (mutually-exclusive flag validation, pre-flight checks, watch/stop modes) BEFORE calling into the launcher. Ralph delegates directly.\n\nThis creates two problems:\n1. **Ralph is inconsistent:** its cmd handler just calls `almanac_loop_launch ralph \"$@\"` and lets the launcher handle everything.\n2. **Harden and converge duplicate validation:** their cmd handlers parse and validate `--watch`, `--stop`, `--goal`, `--approve`, `--fix`, `--loop` flags, but the launcher *also* knows about some of these concepts (e.g., converge's launcher parses `--goal`).\n\nThe split is: cmd handlers handle \"meta-actions\" (--watch, --stop, --loop, --approve, --fix), and the launcher handles \"config actions\" (PRD, mode, provider, model, effort, etc.). But converge's launcher *also* parses `--goal`, which suggests the seam is leaky.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf the cmd handlers' arg parsing were deleted, the calls would route directly to the launcher with all flags intact. The launcher would then need to be smart enough to detect meta-actions (--watch, --stop, --loop) and either handle them or delegate back. This would surface the fact that there are TWO distinct flows hidden in one command:\n- **Flow 1:** launch a new run (config -> exec runner)\n- **Flow 2:** act on an existing run (watch/stop/steer/approve)\n\nCurrently these flows are tangled in the cmd handlers and launcher.\n\n**Rough size:** Multi-file refactor (moderate)  \n- Extract meta-action dispatch (--watch, --stop, --loop, --approve, --fix, --resume) into one place (maybe `lib/loop-cli-dispatch.sh` or a helper in each cmd handler)\n- Make the launcher focus only on config-and-exec (new runs)\n- For converge, reconcile whether `--goal` belongs in the launcher (for new runs) or the CLI (for existing runs)\n\n**Impact:** Clears the seam between \"launch a new run\" and \"interact with existing runs\"; makes ralph's simplicity the pattern, not the exception.\n\n---\n\n### **4. Shallow wrapper: almanac_harden_control_file and almanac_converge_control_file**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `lib/harden-core.sh:1599-1606`  \n- `lib/converge-core.sh:46-53`  \n\n**Problem:**  \nBoth define nearly identical helpers:\n```bash\nalmanac_<loop>_control_file() {\n  local root=\"$1\" kind=\"$2\" name\n  name=\"$(almanac_loop_run_signal_file <loop> \"$kind\")\" || return 1\n  printf '%s/%s\\n' \"$root\" \"$name\"\n}\n```\n\nThese are one-liners that combine two operations: get the signal-file basename (via the loop adapter), then prepend a root path. Callers invoke them directly instead of using the lower-level `almanac_loop_run_signal_file` + path construction.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf deleted, callers would call `almanac_loop_run_signal_file` directly and construct the path manually (2-3 lines instead of 1). But that's not simpler—it's more verbose. These helpers exist for UX. However, the fact that they're nearly identical in both loops suggests a generic factory should live in `lib/run.sh` or `lib/loops.sh`, not redeclared per-loop.\n\n**Rough size:** One-liner refactor  \n- Extract `almanac_loop_control_file <type> <root> <kind>` in `lib/run.sh` (where `almanac_loop_run_signal_file` already lives)\n- Harden and converge call the generic version directly instead of their own wrapper.\n\n**Impact:** Reduces 14 lines of duplication; makes the abstraction explicit (the generic seam owns this, not each loop).\n\n---\n\n### **5. Test coverage gap: shallow-wrapper detection in test-loops.sh**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `tests/test-loops.sh:1-140` (tests the adapter seam)  \n\n**Problem:**  \nThe test file validates that:\n- Loop adapters are auto-discovered (✓)\n- The dispatch machinery works (✓)\n- Each adapter's `exec_argv` and `signal_file` contracts are callable (✓)\n\nBUT it does NOT test for **duplication patterns** or **unintended shallow wrappers**. Specifically:\n- It doesn't assert that `signal_file` across adapters have identical structure (so a later refactoring is safe)\n- It doesn't validate that converge's `exec_argv` uses the same pattern as harden (both invoke `bin/almanac` with the CLI adapter, while ralph uses standalone runner scripts)\n\n**Deletion test:** ✗ **DOES NOT PASS (but it's a test gap, not code duplication)**  \nDeleting these tests doesn't consolidate complexity—it just removes safety checks. The gap is real: tests should enforce the \"expected duplication\" (signal_file's identical case structure) so future changes don't accidentally break the pattern.\n\n**Rough size:** Test addition (small)  \n- Add a test that asserts `signal_file` outputs for stop/steer across ralph, harden, converge follow the same pattern\n- Add a test that validates `exec_argv` for harden and converge both invoke `bin/almanac` (vs. ralph's standalone scripts)\n\n**Impact:** Prevents future refactoring regressions; documents the expected pattern; makes the duplication explicit in tests so maintainers see it.\n\n---\n\n### **6. Interface leakage: skill path references in CLAUDE.md (SKILLS NAVIGATION ISSUE)**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `skills/loop/ralph-loop/SKILL.md:61, 203, 206, 209, 215-219`  \n- `lib/loops/ralph.sh:21` (the authoritative path lives here)\n\n**Problem:**  \nThe skill's SKILL.md instructs users to resolve script paths in this order:\n```\n1. ~/.agents/skills/almanac/ralph-loop/scripts (Codex)\n2. ~/.claude/skills/almanac/ralph-loop/scripts (Claude Code)\n3. ${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts (fallback)\n4. $ALMANAC_HOME/skills/ralph-loop/scripts (repo)\n```\n\nBut the **adapter** (the authoritative source) hardcodes:\n```bash\nlocal scripts=\"$ALMANAC_HOME/skills/loop/ralph-loop/scripts\"\n```\n\n**Discrepancy:**  \n- The skill docs say to look at `ralph-loop/scripts` (correct)\n- But option #4 says `skills/ralph-loop/scripts` (missing the `loop/` subdir)\n- The adapter uses `$ALMANAC_HOME/skills/loop/ralph-loop/scripts` (correct)\n\nThis creates confusion: users following the fallback path (option 4 in the docs) will get \"command not found\" because the path is wrong.\n\nAdditionally, the CONTEXT.md says \"reference paths in CLAUDE.md are wrong — should be absolute\", and commit `e419178` (\"fix(skills,cli): resolve CLAUDE.md offender paths and source dep\") addressed this in recent skill docs. But the inline instructions in `SKILL.md` within the skill itself (not CLAUDE.md) still have stale examples.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✗ **NOT APPLICABLE (documentation issue, not code duplication)**  \nThis is a documentation-code sync issue, not a duplication problem.\n\n**Rough size:** Documentation fix (very small)  \n- Update `SKILL.md:220` to say `$ALMANAC_HOME/skills/loop/ralph-loop/scripts` (add `loop/`)\n- Similarly check harden-loop and converge-loop SHY.md files for the same issue\n\n**Impact:** Eliminates user confusion and copy-paste errors when following the fallback path.\n\n---\n\n### **7. Inconsistent default provider selection in converge vs. harden-core**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `lib/harden-core.sh:105` (calls with `\"claude\"` as default)  \n- `lib/converge-core.sh:84` (calls with `\"$(almanac_provider_default)\"` as default)  \n\n**Problem:**  \nWhen resolving role config, harden hardcodes `claude` as the provider default:\n```bash\nalmanac_loop_role_config \"harden\" \"$role\" \"$lens\" \"claude\" \"\" \"\"\n```\n\nConverge uses the dynamic default:\n```bash\nalmanac_loop_role_config \"converge\" \"$role\" \"\" \"$(almanac_provider_default)\" \"\" \"\"\n```\n\nCONTEXT.md says:\n> **default selection** policy: the active-env provider if available, else the first available in preference order (`claude` → `codex`).\n\nThis means converge's approach (using `almanac_provider_default`) is the correct, flexible one. Harden's hardcoded `claude` is a latent bug: if a user has only codex installed (no claude), harden will try to use an unavailable provider.\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf harden's hardcoded `claude` is removed and replaced with dynamic selection, the behavior becomes **more flexible**—not less. Harden works without claude installed.\n\n**Rough size:** One-line fix  \n```bash\n# lib/harden-core.sh:105 — change from:\nalmanac_loop_role_config \"harden\" \"$role\" \"$lens\" \"claude\" \"\" \"\"\n# to:\nalmanac_loop_role_config \"harden\" \"$role\" \"$lens\" \"$(almanac_provider_default)\" \"\" \"\"\n```\n\n**Impact:** Fixes a latent bug (harden fails gracefully if claude is absent); aligns harden with converge's correct default policy.\n\n---\n\n### **8. Missing abstraction: loop-specific default values embedded in cmd handlers**\n\n**Files involved:**  \n- `cmd/converge.sh:23-25` (converge sets its own defaults)  \n- `lib/loop-launcher.sh:152, 206-207` (launcher re-prompts with defaults)  \n- No explicit defaults in cmd/harden.sh or cmd/ralph.sh (they prompt for everything)\n\n**Problem:**  \nConverge sets defaults for `ROUNDS` and `OVERSEE_EVERY` in the cmd handler:\n```bash\nROUNDS=\"${CONVERGE_ROUND_BUDGET:-10}\"\nNO_OVERSEE=0\nOVERSEE_EVERY=\"${CONVERGE_OVERSEE_EVERY:-1}\"\n```\n\nBut ralph and harden do NOT set defaults in their cmd handlers—they let the launcher prompt. This creates an inconsistency:\n- **Ralph:** cmd handler is thin, launcher gathers everything\n- **Harden:** cmd handler is thick (parses flags), launcher gathers the rest\n- **Converge:** cmd handler sets some defaults, launcher gathers the rest\n\nThe defaults should live **once**, in **one place**, ideally owned by the loop adapter or the launcher (not scattered). Currently, if a dev adds a new harden flag, they have to remember:\n1. Parse it in `cmd/harden.sh`\n2. Validate it in `cmd/harden.sh`  \n3. Re-validate it in the launcher's `_almanac_launch_harden`\n4. Add a fallback default somewhere (maybe in the launcher, maybe in the adapter)\n\n**Deletion test:** ✓ **PASSES**  \nIf the defaults in `cmd/converge.sh` were deleted, the launcher would still prompt for them with its own fallbacks. The cmd handler's defaults just pre-fill—they're not essential. However, the fragmentation suggests a missing abstraction: a loop's default-values map should be defined once (maybe in the adapter, maybe in a config file) and consulted by both the cmd handler and the launcher.\n\n**Rough size:** Multi-file refactor (small-moderate)  \n- Extract `almanac_loop_defaults <name>` function in each loop adapter (or a generic one in `lib/loops.sh`)\n- Cmd handlers call it to pre-fill, launcher calls it to know what to suggest when prompted\n- Consolidates: `CONVERGE_ROUND_BUDGET`, `CONVERGE_OVERSEE_EVERY`, `RALPH_NO_OVERSEE`, `HARDEN_ROUND_BUDGET`, etc. into one registry per loop\n\n**Impact:** Centralizes default-value policy; makes it trivial to add a new loop or extend an existing one; reduces flag-handling duplication in cmd handlers.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| # | Friction | Files | Type | Size | Impact | Priority |\n|---|----------|-------|------|------|--------|----------|\n| 1 | Duplicate `signal_file` across adapters | 3 adapters | Duplication | Mod | Critical seam clarity | **HIGH** |\n| 2 | Duplicate role/field pattern | harden-core, converge-core | Duplication | Mod | Consistency across loops | **HIGH** |\n| 3 | Overlapping arg parsing (cmd vs launcher) | cmd/*, loop-launcher.sh | Interface leakage | Mod | Seam clarity (Flow 1 vs 2) | **HIGH** |\n| 4 | Shallow wrappers: `_control_file` | harden-core, converge-core | Duplication | Small | Minor consolidation | **MED** |\n| 5 | Test coverage gap (shallow-wrapper detection) | test-loops.sh | Gap | Small | Pattern enforcement | **MED** |\n| 6 | Skill path docs mismatch | SKILL.md | Docs | Tiny | UX clarity | **MED** |\n| 7 | Inconsistent provider defaults | harden-core vs converge-core | Logic bug | Tiny | Correctness (claude-less case) | **HIGH** |\n| 8 | Loop defaults scattered | cmd/converge.sh vs launcher | Config scattering | Mod | Consistency, extensibility | **MED** |\n\n---\n\n## Notes\n\n- **Candidates that passed deletion test:** 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8 (high-impact refactors)\n- **Candidates that are documentation/test gaps:** 5, 6 (not code duplication, but important for maintainability)\n- **All candidates avoid recent fixes:** slug unification (d6f859c), worker-status record (d6f859c), loop-core split (540c9aa) are already landed and not repeated here.\n- **All candidates have specific line numbers** and code snippets to anchor the discussion."}],"totalDurationMs":129563,"totalTokens":98651,"totalToolUseCount":66,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":854,"cache_read_input_tokens":92993,"output_tokens":4800,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":854},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":4800,"cache_read_input_tokens":92993,"cache_creation_input_tokens":854,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":854,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":34,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":31,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
