{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0139NZZghu21MiKpPGpuJjtb","type":"tool_result","content":"75\t**Supervision dashboard.** A run is supervised through a `gum`-styled redraw dashboard composed from live run state: reviewer status (per lens, with health), the round, findings tallies, rubric progress, and the feedback verdict. Following the PRD's \"render logic is pure\" rule, the composer `almanac_harden_dashboard_rows` is a **pure function** — it reads worker rows from stdin as `id<TAB>provider<TAB>health` TSV and takes the round/budget, findings tally, rubric progress, and feedback verdict as args, returning the printable rows with no file I/O, clock, or terminal, so it is unit-tested across all five fields without a terminal. The gather helpers feed it real state: `almanac_harden_dashboard_worker_rows` reads each worker's `status.tsv` and classifies its health via the shared detector; `almanac_harden_findings_tally` counts the ledger by status (`open=N fixed=N notes=N`, where notes = `rejected-subjective` + `wontfix-per-context`); `almanac_harden_rubric_progress` reports `checked/total` over the `## Acceptance` checklist; and `almanac_harden_latest_run_id` locates the target's most recent run by directory mtime (a glob+mtime scan, not `ls -t | head`, which would trip SIGPIPE under `set -o pipefail`). `almanac_harden_render_dashboard` ties these together and wraps the composed rows in `almanac_loop_ui_render`, which styles them as a rounded `gum` panel on a TTY and **degrades to plain passthrough** when `gum` is absent (or `ALMANAC_NO_GUM=1`, or output is piped) — keeping almanac's near-zero-dependency promise. It is safe to call before any run/worker/rubric state exists: it degrades to an empty reviewer list and zeroed tallies rather than failing.\n76\t\n77\tThe dashboard is **watched live** through `almanac_harden_dashboard_redraw` (the `almanac harden <target> --watch` mode and the hub's \"watch a run\" action): a redraw loop that, each frame, clears the terminal (TTY only, via the shared `almanac_loop_ui_clear` — never polluting piped output) and reprints `almanac_harden_render_dashboard` from the run's live worker/ledger/rubric state, so reviewer health, findings tallies, rubric progress, and the verdict update as the run progresses. Termination is bounded by construction so it can never run forever: a positive frame cap renders exactly that many frames (the unit-test path); off a TTY an uncapped call renders a single frame and returns (so a pipe/script never blocks); on a TTY it redraws until the latest run's workers have all finished (`almanac_harden_run_has_active_worker`, which keys on each round's per-worker `status.tsv` for liveness — the loop-level run-status blob tracks round/verdict) or there is no run to watch. A single worker's live event stream is watched through `almanac_harden_watch_worker` (`--watch-worker <lens|worker-id>`), which resolves the target's most recent run, accepts a bare lens as shorthand for its `reviewer-<lens>` worker, and delegates to the shared `almanac_loop_worker_watch` — tailing the worker's `events.jsonl` live (`tail -f`) on a TTY and printing its current contents once otherwise. The **steer flow** completes the supervision half: the HITL checkpoint's `steer` choice (above) lets the operator redirect or amend a run between rounds, threading a directive into the subsequent rounds' agents.\n78\t\n79\t**Interactive hub.** Bare `almanac` in a TTY (and `almanac hub` always) opens the **hub** (`cmd/hub.sh`) — the front door to every loop, built as a thin reader over the run registry rather than a retrofit. Its read views are **pure registry projections** so they are testable off a terminal and degrade to plain text without gum. `almanac_loop_hub_render <root> <which> [limit]` projects the registry into one of two lists: `running` emits every run whose lifecycle status is still `running`, carrying its live `round`/`summary` from the run-status blob, and surfaces a running entry whose pid is gone as `stale` (via `almanac_loop_run_is_stale`) so a crashed run is visible rather than silently lost; `recent` emits the finished runs (done/failed/aborted) newest-finish-first, capped at `limit` (default 10). `almanac_loop_hub_overview` composes both sections (with empty-state lines) and frames them through the shared `almanac_loop_ui_render`, so the hub shows a rounded gum panel on a TTY and plain text without gum. Off a TTY `cmd/hub.sh` renders that overview once and exits — it never blocks on a menu, so `almanac hub | cat`, scripts, and tests get a clean read view (`tests/test-hub.sh` drives the real `bin/almanac` chain: bare-almanac-off-a-TTY prints help and does *not* open the hub; `almanac hub` renders the running + recent overview from a seeded registry). The **per-run actions** layer on this read view: selecting a running loop offers **watch**, **stop**, and **queue-steer**, each a pure-ish file/signal operation in `lib/run.sh` so it is unit-testable and also drivable non-interactively (`almanac hub --watch|--stop|--steer <id>`, the seam the gum menu and scripts/tests share). `almanac_loop_run_signal_file <type> <kind>` maps a run type to the dot-file its runner watches between rounds (`.ralph-stop`/`.ralph-steer`, `.harden-stop`/`.harden-steer`) — it dispatches to the loop adapter's `signal_file` control verb (see the loop-adapter seam below) rather than branching on type, so each loop owns its own signal files. `almanac_loop_run_stop` writes that stop file under the run's root (its working dir) **and** best-effort `TERM`s a live numeric pid (never a dead/finished one), so a run tears down at its next between-round check or immediately if blocked mid-round. `almanac_loop_run_steer` writes the directive to the steer file the next round consumes (ralph reads `.ralph-steer`), rejecting a blank directive. `almanac_loop_run_detail` is the pure per-run detail render (identity, live status — upgraded to `stale` when a running pid is gone — round/summary, start/finish); `almanac_loop_run_watch` renders it once off a TTY (the scripts-safe path) and redraws it on a `follow` TTY until the run reaches a terminal status. On a TTY, `cmd/hub.sh`'s `almanac_hub_menu` opens a selection menu whose top entry is always **+ New run**, followed by each running loop (each row carries `[id]`) and `quit`; selecting a running loop opens a watch/stop/steer/back action menu over it. The menu opens even with an empty registry (so New run is reachable with no prior runs), and **degrades gracefully without gum**: with gum it is a styled `gum choose`/`gum input`/`gum confirm`, and without gum it renders as a plain numbered menu read over stdin — both built on the shared `almanac_loop_ui_choose`/`almanac_loop_ui_input`/`almanac_loop_ui_confirm` seam, so the same menus work on any TTY. Off a TTY (piped/captured) the hub still renders the read-only overview once and exits. The **new-run** flow is itself built on a pure composer + non-interactive seam, mirroring the per-run actions: `almanac_loop_new_run_argv <type> [key=value…]` composes the launcher argv (one token per line, leading with the `ralph`/`harden` subcommand) — ralph config maps to ralph.sh flags (`--prd`/`--mode`/`--provider`/`--model`/`--effort`/`--iterations`/`--no-oversee`), harden launches the convergence loop (`harden <target> --loop [--rounds N]`); `almanac_loop_new_run_env <type> [key=value…]` composes the `KEY=VALUE` env a run needs beyond its argv (ralph emits none — all flags; harden's reviewer config rides on `HARDEN_LENSES`/`HARDEN_PROVIDER`/`HARDEN_MODEL`/`HARDEN_EFFORT`). Both return 1 for an unknown type and 2 for a missing required field (ralph: prd; harden: target), so a malformed run is rejected rather than launched. `cmd/hub.sh`'s `almanac_hub_launch_new` either prints the resolved `ENV… almanac <argv>` command (dry-run, the scripts-safe preview the gum confirm reuses) or exports the env and execs the launcher, handing over the terminal. The interactive New-run flow (`almanac_hub_new_run`) picks the loop type, then hands off to the shared **almanac launcher** (`almanac_loop_launch`, see the CLI section) — no run-config logic lives in the hub any more; the same launcher backs `almanac ralph` / `almanac harden`, so the New-run UX is identical to running those directly. (The non-interactive `--new` composer seam below stays for scripts/tests.) The seam is also drivable directly: `almanac hub --new <ralph|harden> [config…] [--dry-run]`. `tests/test-hub.sh` pins the non-interactive seams (`--steer` writes the steer file with the directive, `--stop` writes the stop file, `--watch` renders the run detail once off a TTY, `--new … --dry-run` composes the ralph/harden launch and rejects a run missing its required config).\n80\t\n81\tThe `harden-loop` skill (`skills/loop/harden-loop/SKILL.md`) is the agent-facing companion to the harden CLI - it teaches the convergence contract, the round structure, lenses, per-role provider config, supervision, and gum-optional degradation, pointing at `almanac harden <target>`. The `converge-loop` skill (`skills/loop/converge-loop/SKILL.md`) is the companion to `almanac converge` - it teaches the generic goal/exec loop, mutable goal state, overseer contract, role config, supervision, and when to choose converge instead of ralph or harden. Like `ralph-loop`, both are `disable-model-invocation: true` heavyweight autonomous loops: user-invocable via `/almanac:harden-loop` or `/almanac:converge-loop`, and loadable by orchestrators without bloating the auto-listing.\n82\t\n83\t## Shared Loop Engine (`lib/run.sh`, `lib/worker.sh` + focused modules)\n84\t\n85\tThe loop behavior Ralph, harden-loop, and converge-loop build on is split across **focused modules**, each its own test surface — the old `lib/loop-core.sh` god-module was **deleted** (loop-engine-split slice 09), not kept as a barrel, and every caller sources only the modules it uses. `lib/run.sh` owns the run registry + run-status record + control (stop/steer/watch) + worker health + the hub's read views; `lib/worker.sh` owns the worker orchestration (background fan-out); the UI seam, role config, agent shapes, provider/loop adapters, and feedback each live in their own file (below). The **feedback engine** lives in its own module, **`lib/feedback.sh`** (extracted from the engine in loop-engine-split slice 07): feedback detection (`almanac_loop_feedback_commands`) maps project marker files to commands and renders prompt-ready markdown (`almanac_loop_feedback_markdown`); the **feedback runner** (`almanac_loop_feedback_run`) is its execution half — it runs each detected loop from the project root, emits one `<command>\\t<pass|fail>` verdict row per loop, and returns a non-zero aggregate when any loop failed, so a caller can gate on the aggregate while reading per-loop verdicts. `lib/feedback.sh` is self-contained (no `lib/core.sh` dependency — only printf/tr/mkdir/eval) and its own test surface (`tests/test-feedback.sh`: the marker-file→command mapping and the per-loop verdict + aggregate exit code). The engine modules do not use feedback internally; its consumers source `lib/feedback.sh` **directly** — Ralph's `prompt.sh` for the detection markdown (its only engine dependency), and `lib/harden-core.sh`'s fixer gate (`almanac_harden_report_feedback`) for the runner — rather than borrowing it transitively. Role config resolution lives in its own module, **`lib/role.sh`** (extracted from the engine in loop-engine-split slice 06): `almanac_loop_role_field` layers lens-specific, role-specific, consumer-wide, then default provider/model/effort values, so harden reviewer lenses can mix providers, and `almanac_loop_role_config` projects a role's full tuple as three TSV rows. `lib/role.sh` is self-contained (no `lib/core.sh` dependency — only printf/tr/sed + bash indirect expansion) and its own test surface (`tests/test-role.sh`: the lens→role→consumer-wide→default precedence, the set-empty-wins rule, env-key normalisation, and the role_config projection). The engine modules do not use role config internally; its consumers source `lib/role.sh` **directly** (`once.sh`/`afk.sh` for the iteration agent, `lib/harden-core.sh` for its three roles, `lib/converge-core.sh` for its agent and overseer roles) rather than borrowing it transitively. **Ralph resolves its iteration-agent provider/model/effort through this same shared helper** (`once.sh` and `afk.sh` call `almanac_loop_role_field ralph agent …`): the bare `RALPH_PROVIDER` / `RALPH_MODEL` / `RALPH_EFFORT` still work as the consumer-wide fallback, and new per-role `RALPH_AGENT_*` keys now override them. Provider config still falls through to ralph's own CLI auto-detection (`CODEX_THREAD_ID`, then the installed CLI) when no key is set — that runtime detection is ralph-specific, not a role-config concern. afk's overseer reuses the iteration agent's role-resolved model/effort (as before), now passed straight to the shared seam (a non-stream, read-only call) rather than shaped into local arg arrays. The shared **agent run shapes** live in `lib/agent.sh` (moved out of `loop-core.sh` in loop-engine-split slice 04) as three intention-revealing functions that replace the old mode-parametric `agent_run`: each takes the common quartet `(provider, model, effort, sandbox)` plus a prompt file and a result file, consumes the provider adapter's argv + filter, and owns only execution mechanics — so a call reads as intent and there are no invalid mode combinations to guard. **`almanac_loop_agent_capture`** runs the provider silently, capturing the raw event stream to its events file and (for adapters with an `*_extract` hook) the final result to the result file via a direct redirect that keeps the provider's exit; harden's fan-out / ratify / fixer, ralph's overseer, and converge's worker/overseer calls use it. **`almanac_loop_agent_stream`** additionally tees that raw stream to the events file *and* pipes the adapter's filtered live assistant text to stdout, preserving the producer's exit across the tee/jq pipe via `PIPESTATUS` (a provider failure still propagates instead of being masked by the filter) and never echoing the events-file path (it would corrupt the live stream); an optional trailing **merge-stderr** flag folds the provider's stderr into the captured/streamed output (`2>&1`) so a caller that relies on stderr-in-log — ralph `once.sh`'s `codex ... 2>&1 | tee` — keeps that capture, default leaving stderr on the terminal. **`almanac_loop_agent_raw`** is the native-passthrough shape for adapters that declare `*_supports_raw` (codex): it runs *without* `--json` so codex's native output streams straight to the terminal (no jq filter, no events capture), while `--output-last-message` still captures the result and the direct exec propagates codex's exit — ralph's `RALPH_CODEX_VERBOSE` mode; a provider without raw support degrades to a silent capture so output is never dropped. Each shape is its own test surface in `tests/test-agent.sh` (per-shape behavior behind a fake provider — argv shaping, the sandbox→permission mapping, stream filtering, `PIPESTATUS` propagation, and the raw/degrade paths). The routing has landed for **both `once.sh` providers**: its **claude** invocation runs through `almanac_loop_agent_stream`, and its **default codex** invocation now does too (stream + merge-stderr, so the session log still captures the raw `--json` stream *and* codex's stderr) — neither is an inline provider exec any more, and both gain the seam's `PIPESTATUS` failure propagation (the claude path also gained a real failure surface, since its old pipe had no `pipefail`). `once.sh`'s `RALPH_CODEX_VERBOSE` raw-output branch now routes through the seam too, via its **raw** passthrough mode (no `--json`, native output to the terminal); the result-print and failure-tail are thin ralph post-steps around the seam call. With both providers and the verbose branch routed, `once.sh` no longer builds `MODEL_ARG`/`EFFORT_ARG` arrays or runs any inline provider exec — that arg-shaping lives in the seam. The same routing has now landed for `afk.sh`'s **default codex** invocation: it runs through `almanac_loop_agent_stream` in merge-stderr mode (so the per-iteration session log still captures the raw `--json` stream *and* codex's stderr), the seam's `--output-last-message` target is afk's `$tmpfile` so the `<promise>` extraction reads the result back unchanged. `afk.sh`'s **claude** invocation now routes too: the seam gained a **permission-mode opt-out** — a `default` sandbox sentinel makes `almanac_loop_agent_claude_permission` return an empty mode so `--permission-mode` is omitted entirely (claude's own default), preserving afk's iteration agent, which (unlike `once.sh`, which uses `acceptEdits`) has never set one. afk passes its `$tmpfile` as the seam's result file and reads the extracted result back for `<promise>` extraction (as its codex path does), and gains the seam's `PIPESTATUS` failure propagation — its old inline pipe had no `pipefail`, so claude failures were swallowed and the run was falsely marked done. With this, **every ralph provider invocation — both iteration providers in both `once.sh` and `afk.sh`, each script's `RALPH_CODEX_VERBOSE` raw-output branch, AND afk's overseer read-only judge call — now routes through the shared seam** (via its raw passthrough mode for the verbose branches): **no inline provider exec remains in either launcher (#66 criterion 6)**. The overseer's call is a **non-stream** seam call with the `read-only` sandbox (→ claude `--permission-mode plan` / codex `--sandbox read-only`, the read-only modes it used inline) — the overseer parses the verdict rather than streaming it, so the seam captures the model's response to a result file the overseer reads back; it reuses the iteration agent's role-resolved `AGENT_MODEL`/`AGENT_EFFORT` (its model/effort today) and is guarded with `|| true` so a flaky/missing provider can never kill the overseer subshell. With the overseer routed, `afk.sh` has dropped its `MODEL_ARG`/`EFFORT_ARG` arrays too, reaching parity with `once.sh` — provider arg-shaping now lives only in the seam. The migrated `almanac ralph` is verified end-to-end by `tests/test-ralph-smoke.sh`, which drives the full user-facing chain (`bin/almanac` → `cmd/ralph.sh` → the launcher → `once.sh`/`afk.sh`) behind a fake provider — asserting a once run registers and completes, afk honors the iteration budget, afk stops on the agent's `<promise>COMPLETE</promise>`, the overseer is wired on by default (off with `--no-oversee`), and afk pushes the agent's commit to the remote at end-of-loop — so the loop's iteration, commit, and overseer behavior are unchanged by the migration (#66 criterion 5). **#66 (ralph migration onto the shared engine) is complete.** Worker orchestration (`lib/worker.sh`) starts provider runs in the background through that runner and records per-worker `status.tsv`, `events.jsonl`, `result.txt`, and `stderr.log` files under the run directory. The run registry records launched loops under `.almanac/runs/` in the caller repo so multiple runs (harden, ralph, converge) can be listed and monitored from one place. `index.tsv` is the lightweight list pointer — id/type/target/pid/status-file/start/status, one row per run — and each run also gets a `status.tsv` **blob** that is the per-run detail view. The blob carries the same identity plus the live progress the index does not: `round` (harden round / ralph iteration / converge tick) and `summary` (a worker/lens/goal summary line), updated mid-run via `almanac_loop_update_run_progress`. On exit a run is marked `done`, `failed`, or `aborted` with a finish time (`almanac_loop_mark_run_status`); a still-`running` entry whose pid is gone is detectable as stale via `almanac_loop_run_is_stale` (so a crashed run can be surfaced/reaped). The hub-facing read API is `almanac_loop_list_runs` (all index rows, header stripped) and `almanac_loop_read_run` (one run's full blob). The blob is the **run-status record**, and its schema is the **single run-status contract** — identical for harden, ralph, and converge **by construction**. The record module **`lib/run.sh`** (extracted in loop-engine-split slice 05) owns the canonical field list (`id`, `type`, `target`, `pid`, `status_file`, `started_at`, `status`, `finished_at`, `round`, `summary`) in one place — `almanac_loop_record_fields` — and exposes set/get-by-name accessors: `almanac_loop_record_get <file> <field>` reads a field, and `almanac_loop_record_set <file> field=value…` rewrites the record with the full canonical key set, preserving every field a caller does not name (and rejecting a non-canonical field). So `register` seeds the record by naming only its launch fields, `mark`/`update` change only their lifecycle/progress fields, and **no caller enumerates the schema** — the identical-key-set guarantee is structural, not asserted after the fact (the index row remains the lightweight pointer: the record's first seven fields, `id`/`type`/`target`/`pid`/`status_file`/`started_at`/`status`). `lib/run.sh` is self-contained (no `lib/core.sh` dependency) and its own test surface (`tests/test-run.sh`: schema, set/get-by-name, round-trip-preserving write, unknown-field rejection, and the identical-key-set-by-construction contract); `almanac_loop_status_field` is now a thin alias over `almanac_loop_record_get` (both in `lib/run.sh`). `ralph-loop` launchers source `ralph-run-registry.sh`; `once.sh` and `afk.sh` register the PRD run before starting providers, call `ralph_update_run_progress` (a guarded wrapper over `almanac_loop_update_run_progress`) at the start of each iteration to emit the live `round` (iteration number) + `summary`, and mark it at process exit — so a running ralph shows live iteration progress in the hub through the same run-status contract harden emits, not just a start/finish marker. On the harden side, `almanac_harden_run` registers the loop-level run at start (`type=harden`), calls `almanac_loop_update_run_progress` each round (round + lens/open-blocking summary), and finalizes it through `almanac_harden_run_finalize` — `done` on converge or ship, `failed` at the round budget, `aborted` on a signal or mid-round `_die` (via an `EXIT`/`INT`/`TERM` trap) — so a harden run appears in the hub with the same lifecycle as ralph. On the converge side, `almanac_converge_run` registers `type=converge`, updates round progress as `goal=<slug>`, and finalizes done/failed/aborted through the same registry contract. `.almanac/` is ignored because registry state is runtime-only.\n86\t\n87\tThe **gum-or-plain UI seam** every consumer's dashboard composes from lives in its own module, **`lib/ui.sh`** — extracted from the engine (loop-engine-split slice 02) so each consumer (`lib/loop-launcher.sh`, `lib/harden-core.sh`, `cmd/hub.sh`, and `lib/run.sh` for the hub read views) sources it **directly** rather than borrowing it transitively; `lib/ui.sh` is self-contained (no dependency on `lib/core.sh`) and is its own test surface (`tests/test-ui.sh`). `almanac_loop_ui_has_gum` is the single gate for `gum` styling — true only when `gum` is installed, stdout is a TTY, and `ALMANAC_NO_GUM` is unset — so styled output is opt-out and never appears in piped/captured streams; `almanac_loop_ui_render` styles a stdin block as a rounded panel when that gate is open and passes it through unchanged otherwise (graceful degradation); `almanac_loop_ui_status_glyph` is a pure state→glyph map; and `almanac_loop_ui_clear` clears the terminal between redraw-loop frames only when stdout is a TTY (a no-op off a terminal, so it never pollutes piped/captured output). It also owns the **interactive selection primitives** the hub menus are built on — `almanac_loop_ui_choose` (pick one of N options), `almanac_loop_ui_input` (free-text value with optional default), and `almanac_loop_ui_confirm` (yes/no) — each gum-styled (`gum choose`/`gum input`/`gum confirm`) when `almanac_loop_ui_has_gum` is open and degrading to a plain numbered menu / `read` prompt otherwise, so menu-driven flows work on any TTY without gum. The selection *logic* is split into pure, unit-tested helpers — `almanac_loop_ui_menu_render` (options → numbered list) and `almanac_loop_ui_menu_pick` (typed number → chosen option, rejecting blank/non-numeric/out-of-range) — with the choose/input/confirm wrappers adding only the gum-or-`read` I/O around them; the prompt goes to stderr so callers capture only the choice on stdout. `lib/worker.sh` also owns `almanac_loop_worker_watch`, which streams one worker's live `events.jsonl` so any consumer can watch a single reviewer/fixer — reading the worker's events-file path from its `status.tsv`, tailing it live (`tail -f`) on a TTY and printing its current contents once otherwise (so pipes and tests never block). **Worker health detection** (`lib/run.sh`) classifies a reviewer's progress so the dashboard can surface a stalled (log stopped advancing), idle (running but never produced an event), or looping (same event repeating) worker: `almanac_loop_worker_health` is a pure predicate over `(status, log-age, event-count, trailing-repeat, stall-threshold, loop-threshold) → running|stalled|idle|looping|done|failed` (table-tested with no files/clock), fed by the gather wrapper `almanac_loop_worker_health_of` (with `almanac_loop_file_age_secs`, `almanac_loop_count_lines`, and `almanac_loop_trailing_repeat` measuring the worker's `events.jsonl`).\n88\t\n89\tThe **provider-adapter seam** every consumer's provider knowledge routes through lives in **`lib/agent.sh`** + **`lib/providers/<name>.sh`** — extracted from the engine (loop-engine-split slice 03). Each backend is a drop-in adapter file (`lib/providers/codex.sh`, `lib/providers/claude.sh`) implementing one fixed contract — `available`, `active_env`, `models`, `efforts`, `display`, `argv`, `filter` — and adapters are **auto-discovered**: the provider list IS the files present (`almanac_provider_list`), so there is no central provider list to keep in sync. The seam is **deep on invocation**: the adapter's `argv` yields the full exec tokens — including the sandbox→permission mapping (claude's `--permission-mode`) and `--json`-or-not by shape — into `_ALMANAC_AGENT_ARGV`, and its `filter` yields the jq event-stream program, so **no `codex …`/`claude …` literal lives outside an adapter**. `lib/agent.sh` owns discovery, name-normalising dispatch (`almanac_provider_call <name> <verb>` — the one place that turns a provider name into a function call, so no caller ever branches on provider name), and the single central remainder: the **default-selection policy** (`almanac_provider_default` — the active-env provider if installed, else the first installed in preference order claude → codex), which unifies ralph's runtime detection (`once.sh`/`afk.sh` `detect_provider`) with the launcher's provider menu so they agree. Self-contained like `lib/ui.sh` (no dependency on `lib/core.sh`), it is its own test surface (`tests/test-providers.sh`, exercising discovery, availability, active-env, menus, the codex/claude `argv` shapes, the sandbox→permission mapping, and default-selection — all behind a fake `command -v`, no real model calls). Consumers source it **directly**: the agent run shapes in `lib/agent.sh` (which dispatch argv + filter through the seam and own only execution mechanics; consumed by `lib/worker.sh`'s worker fan-out, `lib/harden-core.sh`, and ralph's `once.sh`/`afk.sh`), `lib/loop-launcher.sh` (its provider menu / availability / list), and ralph's `once.sh`/`afk.sh` (detection / availability / display name).\n90\t\n91\tThe **loop-adapter seam** mirrors the provider one for loops, and lives in **`lib/loops.sh`** + **`lib/loops/<name>.sh`** — extracted from the engine (loop-engine-split slice 08). Each loop is a drop-in adapter file (`lib/loops/ralph.sh`, `lib/loops/harden.sh`, `lib/loops/converge.sh`) and adapters are **auto-discovered**: the loop list IS the files present (`almanac_loop_adapter_list`), so there is no central loop list to keep in sync. `lib/loops.sh` owns discovery and name-normalising dispatch (`almanac_loop_adapter_call <name> <verb>` — the one place a loop type becomes a function call, so no caller branches on loop type). Adapters can declare **launch** — `exec_argv` populates `_ALMANAC_LOOP_ARGV` with the runner's exec tokens (ralph's `mode`→`once.sh`/`afk.sh` under the ralph-loop skill; harden's `almanac harden <target> --loop [--rounds N]`), so the **runner path lives in the adapter, not the launcher**. Adapters also declare **control** — `signal_file <stop|steer>` returns the between-round dot-file basename (`.ralph-stop`/`.ralph-steer`, `.harden-stop`/`.harden-steer`, `.converge-stop`/`.converge-steer`). Converge launches directly through `almanac converge --goal ... --exec ...` in v1, so its adapter exposes the control contract only. The **launcher** (`lib/loop-launcher.sh`) consumes launch — `_almanac_launch_ralph`/`_almanac_launch_harden` exec their runner via `almanac_loop_adapter_call <type> exec_argv …` rather than a hard-coded path — and the **hub** consumes control through `almanac_loop_run_signal_file` (above). Self-contained like `lib/agent.sh` (no dependency on `lib/core.sh` — only printf/tr/basename/source), it is its own test surface (`tests/test-loops.sh`: discovery, dispatch rejecting an unimplemented verb, the `signal_file` control mapping, and the `exec_argv` launch composition for loops against a fake `$ALMANAC_HOME`). Adding a loop is one new file under `lib/loops/`; nothing central changes.\n92\t\n93\t## Validation (`lib/almanac-core.sh`)\n94\t\n95\t`almanac_validate_skill()` checks against the Agent Skills Open Standard:\n96\t- Name format (regex, length, no consecutive hyphens, matches directory)\n97\t- Description presence + length (≤220 chars to keep auto-listing compact)\n98\t- Description starts with `Use when` (a leading YAML quote is stripped first) — agents under-trigger without an explicit trigger up front; the rule is in the validator, with a negative case in `tests/test-skills.sh`\n99\t- Frontmatter size"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f32d037-1d78-4726-b16f-75668d38d257","uuid":"b7c1be4b-7cb2-4a5f-a873-b4e0ce5a2718","timestamp":"2026-05-26T14:34:17.804Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md","content":"**Supervision dashboard.** A run is supervised through a `gum`-styled redraw dashboard composed from live run state: reviewer status (per lens, with health), the round, findings tallies, rubric progress, and the feedback verdict. Following the PRD's \"render logic is pure\" rule, the composer `almanac_harden_dashboard_rows` is a **pure function** — it reads worker rows from stdin as `id<TAB>provider<TAB>health` TSV and takes the round/budget, findings tally, rubric progress, and feedback verdict as args, returning the printable rows with no file I/O, clock, or terminal, so it is unit-tested across all five fields without a terminal. The gather helpers feed it real state: `almanac_harden_dashboard_worker_rows` reads each worker's `status.tsv` and classifies its health via the shared detector; `almanac_harden_findings_tally` counts the ledger by status (`open=N fixed=N notes=N`, where notes = `rejected-subjective` + `wontfix-per-context`); `almanac_harden_rubric_progress` reports `checked/total` over the `## Acceptance` checklist; and `almanac_harden_latest_run_id` locates the target's most recent run by directory mtime (a glob+mtime scan, not `ls -t | head`, which would trip SIGPIPE under `set -o pipefail`). `almanac_harden_render_dashboard` ties these together and wraps the composed rows in `almanac_loop_ui_render`, which styles them as a rounded `gum` panel on a TTY and **degrades to plain passthrough** when `gum` is absent (or `ALMANAC_NO_GUM=1`, or output is piped) — keeping almanac's near-zero-dependency promise. It is safe to call before any run/worker/rubric state exists: it degrades to an empty reviewer list and zeroed tallies rather than failing.\n\nThe dashboard is **watched live** through `almanac_harden_dashboard_redraw` (the `almanac harden <target> --watch` mode and the hub's \"watch a run\" action): a redraw loop that, each frame, clears the terminal (TTY only, via the shared `almanac_loop_ui_clear` — never polluting piped output) and reprints `almanac_harden_render_dashboard` from the run's live worker/ledger/rubric state, so reviewer health, findings tallies, rubric progress, and the verdict update as the run progresses. Termination is bounded by construction so it can never run forever: a positive frame cap renders exactly that many frames (the unit-test path); off a TTY an uncapped call renders a single frame and returns (so a pipe/script never blocks); on a TTY it redraws until the latest run's workers have all finished (`almanac_harden_run_has_active_worker`, which keys on each round's per-worker `status.tsv` for liveness — the loop-level run-status blob tracks round/verdict) or there is no run to watch. A single worker's live event stream is watched through `almanac_harden_watch_worker` (`--watch-worker <lens|worker-id>`), which resolves the target's most recent run, accepts a bare lens as shorthand for its `reviewer-<lens>` worker, and delegates to the shared `almanac_loop_worker_watch` — tailing the worker's `events.jsonl` live (`tail -f`) on a TTY and printing its current contents once otherwise. The **steer flow** completes the supervision half: the HITL checkpoint's `steer` choice (above) lets the operator redirect or amend a run between rounds, threading a directive into the subsequent rounds' agents.\n\n**Interactive hub.** Bare `almanac` in a TTY (and `almanac hub` always) opens the **hub** (`cmd/hub.sh`) — the front door to every loop, built as a thin reader over the run registry rather than a retrofit. Its read views are **pure registry projections** so they are testable off a terminal and degrade to plain text without gum. `almanac_loop_hub_render <root> <which> [limit]` projects the registry into one of two lists: `running` emits every run whose lifecycle status is still `running`, carrying its live `round`/`summary` from the run-status blob, and surfaces a running entry whose pid is gone as `stale` (via `almanac_loop_run_is_stale`) so a crashed run is visible rather than silently lost; `recent` emits the finished runs (done/failed/aborted) newest-finish-first, capped at `limit` (default 10). `almanac_loop_hub_overview` composes both sections (with empty-state lines) and frames them through the shared `almanac_loop_ui_render`, so the hub shows a rounded gum panel on a TTY and plain text without gum. Off a TTY `cmd/hub.sh` renders that overview once and exits — it never blocks on a menu, so `almanac hub | cat`, scripts, and tests get a clean read view (`tests/test-hub.sh` drives the real `bin/almanac` chain: bare-almanac-off-a-TTY prints help and does *not* open the hub; `almanac hub` renders the running + recent overview from a seeded registry). The **per-run actions** layer on this read view: selecting a running loop offers **watch**, **stop**, and **queue-steer**, each a pure-ish file/signal operation in `lib/run.sh` so it is unit-testable and also drivable non-interactively (`almanac hub --watch|--stop|--steer <id>`, the seam the gum menu and scripts/tests share). `almanac_loop_run_signal_file <type> <kind>` maps a run type to the dot-file its runner watches between rounds (`.ralph-stop`/`.ralph-steer`, `.harden-stop`/`.harden-steer`) — it dispatches to the loop adapter's `signal_file` control verb (see the loop-adapter seam below) rather than branching on type, so each loop owns its own signal files. `almanac_loop_run_stop` writes that stop file under the run's root (its working dir) **and** best-effort `TERM`s a live numeric pid (never a dead/finished one), so a run tears down at its next between-round check or immediately if blocked mid-round. `almanac_loop_run_steer` writes the directive to the steer file the next round consumes (ralph reads `.ralph-steer`), rejecting a blank directive. `almanac_loop_run_detail` is the pure per-run detail render (identity, live status — upgraded to `stale` when a running pid is gone — round/summary, start/finish); `almanac_loop_run_watch` renders it once off a TTY (the scripts-safe path) and redraws it on a `follow` TTY until the run reaches a terminal status. On a TTY, `cmd/hub.sh`'s `almanac_hub_menu` opens a selection menu whose top entry is always **+ New run**, followed by each running loop (each row carries `[id]`) and `quit`; selecting a running loop opens a watch/stop/steer/back action menu over it. The menu opens even with an empty registry (so New run is reachable with no prior runs), and **degrades gracefully without gum**: with gum it is a styled `gum choose`/`gum input`/`gum confirm`, and without gum it renders as a plain numbered menu read over stdin — both built on the shared `almanac_loop_ui_choose`/`almanac_loop_ui_input`/`almanac_loop_ui_confirm` seam, so the same menus work on any TTY. Off a TTY (piped/captured) the hub still renders the read-only overview once and exits. The **new-run** flow is itself built on a pure composer + non-interactive seam, mirroring the per-run actions: `almanac_loop_new_run_argv <type> [key=value…]` composes the launcher argv (one token per line, leading with the `ralph`/`harden` subcommand) — ralph config maps to ralph.sh flags (`--prd`/`--mode`/`--provider`/`--model`/`--effort`/`--iterations`/`--no-oversee`), harden launches the convergence loop (`harden <target> --loop [--rounds N]`); `almanac_loop_new_run_env <type> [key=value…]` composes the `KEY=VALUE` env a run needs beyond its argv (ralph emits none — all flags; harden's reviewer config rides on `HARDEN_LENSES`/`HARDEN_PROVIDER`/`HARDEN_MODEL`/`HARDEN_EFFORT`). Both return 1 for an unknown type and 2 for a missing required field (ralph: prd; harden: target), so a malformed run is rejected rather than launched. `cmd/hub.sh`'s `almanac_hub_launch_new` either prints the resolved `ENV… almanac <argv>` command (dry-run, the scripts-safe preview the gum confirm reuses) or exports the env and execs the launcher, handing over the terminal. The interactive New-run flow (`almanac_hub_new_run`) picks the loop type, then hands off to the shared **almanac launcher** (`almanac_loop_launch`, see the CLI section) — no run-config logic lives in the hub any more; the same launcher backs `almanac ralph` / `almanac harden`, so the New-run UX is identical to running those directly. (The non-interactive `--new` composer seam below stays for scripts/tests.) The seam is also drivable directly: `almanac hub --new <ralph|harden> [config…] [--dry-run]`. `tests/test-hub.sh` pins the non-interactive seams (`--steer` writes the steer file with the directive, `--stop` writes the stop file, `--watch` renders the run detail once off a TTY, `--new … --dry-run` composes the ralph/harden launch and rejects a run missing its required config).\n\nThe `harden-loop` skill (`skills/loop/harden-loop/SKILL.md`) is the agent-facing companion to the harden CLI - it teaches the convergence contract, the round structure, lenses, per-role provider config, supervision, and gum-optional degradation, pointing at `almanac harden <target>`. The `converge-loop` skill (`skills/loop/converge-loop/SKILL.md`) is the companion to `almanac converge` - it teaches the generic goal/exec loop, mutable goal state, overseer contract, role config, supervision, and when to choose converge instead of ralph or harden. Like `ralph-loop`, both are `disable-model-invocation: true` heavyweight autonomous loops: user-invocable via `/almanac:harden-loop` or `/almanac:converge-loop`, and loadable by orchestrators without bloating the auto-listing.\n\n## Shared Loop Engine (`lib/run.sh`, `lib/worker.sh` + focused modules)\n\nThe loop behavior Ralph, harden-loop, and converge-loop build on is split across **focused modules**, each its own test surface — the old `lib/loop-core.sh` god-module was **deleted** (loop-engine-split slice 09), not kept as a barrel, and every caller sources only the modules it uses. `lib/run.sh` owns the run registry + run-status record + control (stop/steer/watch) + worker health + the hub's read views; `lib/worker.sh` owns the worker orchestration (background fan-out); the UI seam, role config, agent shapes, provider/loop adapters, and feedback each live in their own file (below). The **feedback engine** lives in its own module, **`lib/feedback.sh`** (extracted from the engine in loop-engine-split slice 07): feedback detection (`almanac_loop_feedback_commands`) maps project marker files to commands and renders prompt-ready markdown (`almanac_loop_feedback_markdown`); the **feedback runner** (`almanac_loop_feedback_run`) is its execution half — it runs each detected loop from the project root, emits one `<command>\\t<pass|fail>` verdict row per loop, and returns a non-zero aggregate when any loop failed, so a caller can gate on the aggregate while reading per-loop verdicts. `lib/feedback.sh` is self-contained (no `lib/core.sh` dependency — only printf/tr/mkdir/eval) and its own test surface (`tests/test-feedback.sh`: the marker-file→command mapping and the per-loop verdict + aggregate exit code). The engine modules do not use feedback internally; its consumers source `lib/feedback.sh` **directly** — Ralph's `prompt.sh` for the detection markdown (its only engine dependency), and `lib/harden-core.sh`'s fixer gate (`almanac_harden_report_feedback`) for the runner — rather than borrowing it transitively. Role config resolution lives in its own module, **`lib/role.sh`** (extracted from the engine in loop-engine-split slice 06): `almanac_loop_role_field` layers lens-specific, role-specific, consumer-wide, then default provider/model/effort values, so harden reviewer lenses can mix providers, and `almanac_loop_role_config` projects a role's full tuple as three TSV rows. `lib/role.sh` is self-contained (no `lib/core.sh` dependency — only printf/tr/sed + bash indirect expansion) and its own test surface (`tests/test-role.sh`: the lens→role→consumer-wide→default precedence, the set-empty-wins rule, env-key normalisation, and the role_config projection). The engine modules do not use role config internally; its consumers source `lib/role.sh` **directly** (`once.sh`/`afk.sh` for the iteration agent, `lib/harden-core.sh` for its three roles, `lib/converge-core.sh` for its agent and overseer roles) rather than borrowing it transitively. **Ralph resolves its iteration-agent provider/model/effort through this same shared helper** (`once.sh` and `afk.sh` call `almanac_loop_role_field ralph agent …`): the bare `RALPH_PROVIDER` / `RALPH_MODEL` / `RALPH_EFFORT` still work as the consumer-wide fallback, and new per-role `RALPH_AGENT_*` keys now override them. Provider config still falls through to ralph's own CLI auto-detection (`CODEX_THREAD_ID`, then the installed CLI) when no key is set — that runtime detection is ralph-specific, not a role-config concern. afk's overseer reuses the iteration agent's role-resolved model/effort (as before), now passed straight to the shared seam (a non-stream, read-only call) rather than shaped into local arg arrays. The shared **agent run shapes** live in `lib/agent.sh` (moved out of `loop-core.sh` in loop-engine-split slice 04) as three intention-revealing functions that replace the old mode-parametric `agent_run`: each takes the common quartet `(provider, model, effort, sandbox)` plus a prompt file and a result file, consumes the provider adapter's argv + filter, and owns only execution mechanics — so a call reads as intent and there are no invalid mode combinations to guard. **`almanac_loop_agent_capture`** runs the provider silently, capturing the raw event stream to its events file and (for adapters with an `*_extract` hook) the final result to the result file via a direct redirect that keeps the provider's exit; harden's fan-out / ratify / fixer, ralph's overseer, and converge's worker/overseer calls use it. **`almanac_loop_agent_stream`** additionally tees that raw stream to the events file *and* pipes the adapter's filtered live assistant text to stdout, preserving the producer's exit across the tee/jq pipe via `PIPESTATUS` (a provider failure still propagates instead of being masked by the filter) and never echoing the events-file path (it would corrupt the live stream); an optional trailing **merge-stderr** flag folds the provider's stderr into the captured/streamed output (`2>&1`) so a caller that relies on stderr-in-log — ralph `once.sh`'s `codex ... 2>&1 | tee` — keeps that capture, default leaving stderr on the terminal. **`almanac_loop_agent_raw`** is the native-passthrough shape for adapters that declare `*_supports_raw` (codex): it runs *without* `--json` so codex's native output streams straight to the terminal (no jq filter, no events capture), while `--output-last-message` still captures the result and the direct exec propagates codex's exit — ralph's `RALPH_CODEX_VERBOSE` mode; a provider without raw support degrades to a silent capture so output is never dropped. Each shape is its own test surface in `tests/test-agent.sh` (per-shape behavior behind a fake provider — argv shaping, the sandbox→permission mapping, stream filtering, `PIPESTATUS` propagation, and the raw/degrade paths). The routing has landed for **both `once.sh` providers**: its **claude** invocation runs through `almanac_loop_agent_stream`, and its **default codex** invocation now does too (stream + merge-stderr, so the session log still captures the raw `--json` stream *and* codex's stderr) — neither is an inline provider exec any more, and both gain the seam's `PIPESTATUS` failure propagation (the claude path also gained a real failure surface, since its old pipe had no `pipefail`). `once.sh`'s `RALPH_CODEX_VERBOSE` raw-output branch now routes through the seam too, via its **raw** passthrough mode (no `--json`, native output to the terminal); the result-print and failure-tail are thin ralph post-steps around the seam call. With both providers and the verbose branch routed, `once.sh` no longer builds `MODEL_ARG`/`EFFORT_ARG` arrays or runs any inline provider exec — that arg-shaping lives in the seam. The same routing has now landed for `afk.sh`'s **default codex** invocation: it runs through `almanac_loop_agent_stream` in merge-stderr mode (so the per-iteration session log still captures the raw `--json` stream *and* codex's stderr), the seam's `--output-last-message` target is afk's `$tmpfile` so the `<promise>` extraction reads the result back unchanged. `afk.sh`'s **claude** invocation now routes too: the seam gained a **permission-mode opt-out** — a `default` sandbox sentinel makes `almanac_loop_agent_claude_permission` return an empty mode so `--permission-mode` is omitted entirely (claude's own default), preserving afk's iteration agent, which (unlike `once.sh`, which uses `acceptEdits`) has never set one. afk passes its `$tmpfile` as the seam's result file and reads the extracted result back for `<promise>` extraction (as its codex path does), and gains the seam's `PIPESTATUS` failure propagation — its old inline pipe had no `pipefail`, so claude failures were swallowed and the run was falsely marked done. With this, **every ralph provider invocation — both iteration providers in both `once.sh` and `afk.sh`, each script's `RALPH_CODEX_VERBOSE` raw-output branch, AND afk's overseer read-only judge call — now routes through the shared seam** (via its raw passthrough mode for the verbose branches): **no inline provider exec remains in either launcher (#66 criterion 6)**. The overseer's call is a **non-stream** seam call with the `read-only` sandbox (→ claude `--permission-mode plan` / codex `--sandbox read-only`, the read-only modes it used inline) — the overseer parses the verdict rather than streaming it, so the seam captures the model's response to a result file the overseer reads back; it reuses the iteration agent's role-resolved `AGENT_MODEL`/`AGENT_EFFORT` (its model/effort today) and is guarded with `|| true` so a flaky/missing provider can never kill the overseer subshell. With the overseer routed, `afk.sh` has dropped its `MODEL_ARG`/`EFFORT_ARG` arrays too, reaching parity with `once.sh` — provider arg-shaping now lives only in the seam. The migrated `almanac ralph` is verified end-to-end by `tests/test-ralph-smoke.sh`, which drives the full user-facing chain (`bin/almanac` → `cmd/ralph.sh` → the launcher → `once.sh`/`afk.sh`) behind a fake provider — asserting a once run registers and completes, afk honors the iteration budget, afk stops on the agent's `<promise>COMPLETE</promise>`, the overseer is wired on by default (off with `--no-oversee`), and afk pushes the agent's commit to the remote at end-of-loop — so the loop's iteration, commit, and overseer behavior are unchanged by the migration (#66 criterion 5). **#66 (ralph migration onto the shared engine) is complete.** Worker orchestration (`lib/worker.sh`) starts provider runs in the background through that runner and records per-worker `status.tsv`, `events.jsonl`, `result.txt`, and `stderr.log` files under the run directory. The run registry records launched loops under `.almanac/runs/` in the caller repo so multiple runs (harden, ralph, converge) can be listed and monitored from one place. `index.tsv` is the lightweight list pointer — id/type/target/pid/status-file/start/status, one row per run — and each run also gets a `status.tsv` **blob** that is the per-run detail view. The blob carries the same identity plus the live progress the index does not: `round` (harden round / ralph iteration / converge tick) and `summary` (a worker/lens/goal summary line), updated mid-run via `almanac_loop_update_run_progress`. On exit a run is marked `done`, `failed`, or `aborted` with a finish time (`almanac_loop_mark_run_status`); a still-`running` entry whose pid is gone is detectable as stale via `almanac_loop_run_is_stale` (so a crashed run can be surfaced/reaped). The hub-facing read API is `almanac_loop_list_runs` (all index rows, header stripped) and `almanac_loop_read_run` (one run's full blob). The blob is the **run-status record**, and its schema is the **single run-status contract** — identical for harden, ralph, and converge **by construction**. The record module **`lib/run.sh`** (extracted in loop-engine-split slice 05) owns the canonical field list (`id`, `type`, `target`, `pid`, `status_file`, `started_at`, `status`, `finished_at`, `round`, `summary`) in one place — `almanac_loop_record_fields` — and exposes set/get-by-name accessors: `almanac_loop_record_get <file> <field>` reads a field, and `almanac_loop_record_set <file> field=value…` rewrites the record with the full canonical key set, preserving every field a caller does not name (and rejecting a non-canonical field). So `register` seeds the record by naming only its launch fields, `mark`/`update` change only their lifecycle/progress fields, and **no caller enumerates the schema** — the identical-key-set guarantee is structural, not asserted after the fact (the index row remains the lightweight pointer: the record's first seven fields, `id`/`type`/`target`/`pid`/`status_file`/`started_at`/`status`). `lib/run.sh` is self-contained (no `lib/core.sh` dependency) and its own test surface (`tests/test-run.sh`: schema, set/get-by-name, round-trip-preserving write, unknown-field rejection, and the identical-key-set-by-construction contract); `almanac_loop_status_field` is now a thin alias over `almanac_loop_record_get` (both in `lib/run.sh`). `ralph-loop` launchers source `ralph-run-registry.sh`; `once.sh` and `afk.sh` register the PRD run before starting providers, call `ralph_update_run_progress` (a guarded wrapper over `almanac_loop_update_run_progress`) at the start of each iteration to emit the live `round` (iteration number) + `summary`, and mark it at process exit — so a running ralph shows live iteration progress in the hub through the same run-status contract harden emits, not just a start/finish marker. On the harden side, `almanac_harden_run` registers the loop-level run at start (`type=harden`), calls `almanac_loop_update_run_progress` each round (round + lens/open-blocking summary), and finalizes it through `almanac_harden_run_finalize` — `done` on converge or ship, `failed` at the round budget, `aborted` on a signal or mid-round `_die` (via an `EXIT`/`INT`/`TERM` trap) — so a harden run appears in the hub with the same lifecycle as ralph. On the converge side, `almanac_converge_run` registers `type=converge`, updates round progress as `goal=<slug>`, and finalizes done/failed/aborted through the same registry contract. `.almanac/` is ignored because registry state is runtime-only.\n\nThe **gum-or-plain UI seam** every consumer's dashboard composes from lives in its own module, **`lib/ui.sh`** — extracted from the engine (loop-engine-split slice 02) so each consumer (`lib/loop-launcher.sh`, `lib/harden-core.sh`, `cmd/hub.sh`, and `lib/run.sh` for the hub read views) sources it **directly** rather than borrowing it transitively; `lib/ui.sh` is self-contained (no dependency on `lib/core.sh`) and is its own test surface (`tests/test-ui.sh`). `almanac_loop_ui_has_gum` is the single gate for `gum` styling — true only when `gum` is installed, stdout is a TTY, and `ALMANAC_NO_GUM` is unset — so styled output is opt-out and never appears in piped/captured streams; `almanac_loop_ui_render` styles a stdin block as a rounded panel when that gate is open and passes it through unchanged otherwise (graceful degradation); `almanac_loop_ui_status_glyph` is a pure state→glyph map; and `almanac_loop_ui_clear` clears the terminal between redraw-loop frames only when stdout is a TTY (a no-op off a terminal, so it never pollutes piped/captured output). It also owns the **interactive selection primitives** the hub menus are built on — `almanac_loop_ui_choose` (pick one of N options), `almanac_loop_ui_input` (free-text value with optional default), and `almanac_loop_ui_confirm` (yes/no) — each gum-styled (`gum choose`/`gum input`/`gum confirm`) when `almanac_loop_ui_has_gum` is open and degrading to a plain numbered menu / `read` prompt otherwise, so menu-driven flows work on any TTY without gum. The selection *logic* is split into pure, unit-tested helpers — `almanac_loop_ui_menu_render` (options → numbered list) and `almanac_loop_ui_menu_pick` (typed number → chosen option, rejecting blank/non-numeric/out-of-range) — with the choose/input/confirm wrappers adding only the gum-or-`read` I/O around them; the prompt goes to stderr so callers capture only the choice on stdout. `lib/worker.sh` also owns `almanac_loop_worker_watch`, which streams one worker's live `events.jsonl` so any consumer can watch a single reviewer/fixer — reading the worker's events-file path from its `status.tsv`, tailing it live (`tail -f`) on a TTY and printing its current contents once otherwise (so pipes and tests never block). **Worker health detection** (`lib/run.sh`) classifies a reviewer's progress so the dashboard can surface a stalled (log stopped advancing), idle (running but never produced an event), or looping (same event repeating) worker: `almanac_loop_worker_health` is a pure predicate over `(status, log-age, event-count, trailing-repeat, stall-threshold, loop-threshold) → running|stalled|idle|looping|done|failed` (table-tested with no files/clock), fed by the gather wrapper `almanac_loop_worker_health_of` (with `almanac_loop_file_age_secs`, `almanac_loop_count_lines`, and `almanac_loop_trailing_repeat` measuring the worker's `events.jsonl`).\n\nThe **provider-adapter seam** every consumer's provider knowledge routes through lives in **`lib/agent.sh`** + **`lib/providers/<name>.sh`** — extracted from the engine (loop-engine-split slice 03). Each backend is a drop-in adapter file (`lib/providers/codex.sh`, `lib/providers/claude.sh`) implementing one fixed contract — `available`, `active_env`, `models`, `efforts`, `display`, `argv`, `filter` — and adapters are **auto-discovered**: the provider list IS the files present (`almanac_provider_list`), so there is no central provider list to keep in sync. The seam is **deep on invocation**: the adapter's `argv` yields the full exec tokens — including the sandbox→permission mapping (claude's `--permission-mode`) and `--json`-or-not by shape — into `_ALMANAC_AGENT_ARGV`, and its `filter` yields the jq event-stream program, so **no `codex …`/`claude …` literal lives outside an adapter**. `lib/agent.sh` owns discovery, name-normalising dispatch (`almanac_provider_call <name> <verb>` — the one place that turns a provider name into a function call, so no caller ever branches on provider name), and the single central remainder: the **default-selection policy** (`almanac_provider_default` — the active-env provider if installed, else the first installed in preference order claude → codex), which unifies ralph's runtime detection (`once.sh`/`afk.sh` `detect_provider`) with the launcher's provider menu so they agree. Self-contained like `lib/ui.sh` (no dependency on `lib/core.sh`), it is its own test surface (`tests/test-providers.sh`, exercising discovery, availability, active-env, menus, the codex/claude `argv` shapes, the sandbox→permission mapping, and default-selection — all behind a fake `command -v`, no real model calls). Consumers source it **directly**: the agent run shapes in `lib/agent.sh` (which dispatch argv + filter through the seam and own only execution mechanics; consumed by `lib/worker.sh`'s worker fan-out, `lib/harden-core.sh`, and ralph's `once.sh`/`afk.sh`), `lib/loop-launcher.sh` (its provider menu / availability / list), and ralph's `once.sh`/`afk.sh` (detection / availability / display name).\n\nThe **loop-adapter seam** mirrors the provider one for loops, and lives in **`lib/loops.sh`** + **`lib/loops/<name>.sh`** — extracted from the engine (loop-engine-split slice 08). Each loop is a drop-in adapter file (`lib/loops/ralph.sh`, `lib/loops/harden.sh`, `lib/loops/converge.sh`) and adapters are **auto-discovered**: the loop list IS the files present (`almanac_loop_adapter_list`), so there is no central loop list to keep in sync. `lib/loops.sh` owns discovery and name-normalising dispatch (`almanac_loop_adapter_call <name> <verb>` — the one place a loop type becomes a function call, so no caller branches on loop type). Adapters can declare **launch** — `exec_argv` populates `_ALMANAC_LOOP_ARGV` with the runner's exec tokens (ralph's `mode`→`once.sh`/`afk.sh` under the ralph-loop skill; harden's `almanac harden <target> --loop [--rounds N]`), so the **runner path lives in the adapter, not the launcher**. Adapters also declare **control** — `signal_file <stop|steer>` returns the between-round dot-file basename (`.ralph-stop`/`.ralph-steer`, `.harden-stop`/`.harden-steer`, `.converge-stop`/`.converge-steer`). Converge launches directly through `almanac converge --goal ... --exec ...` in v1, so its adapter exposes the control contract only. The **launcher** (`lib/loop-launcher.sh`) consumes launch — `_almanac_launch_ralph`/`_almanac_launch_harden` exec their runner via `almanac_loop_adapter_call <type> exec_argv …` rather than a hard-coded path — and the **hub** consumes control through `almanac_loop_run_signal_file` (above). Self-contained like `lib/agent.sh` (no dependency on `lib/core.sh` — only printf/tr/basename/source), it is its own test surface (`tests/test-loops.sh`: discovery, dispatch rejecting an unimplemented verb, the `signal_file` control mapping, and the `exec_argv` launch composition for loops against a fake `$ALMANAC_HOME`). Adding a loop is one new file under `lib/loops/`; nothing central changes.\n\n## Validation (`lib/almanac-core.sh`)\n\n`almanac_validate_skill()` checks against the Agent Skills Open Standard:\n- Name format (regex, length, no consecutive hyphens, matches directory)\n- Description presence + length (≤220 chars to keep auto-listing compact)\n- Description starts with `Use when` (a leading YAML quote is stripped first) — agents under-trigger without an explicit trigger up front; the rule is in the validator, with a negative case in `tests/test-skills.sh`\n- Frontmatter size","numLines":25,"startLine":75,"totalLines":111}}}
