/loops/converge.sh`) and adapters are **auto-discovered**: the loop list IS the files present (`almanac_loop_adapter_list`), so there is no central loop list to keep in sync. `lib/loops.sh` owns discovery and name-normalising dispatch (`almanac_loop_adapter_call <name> <verb>` — the one place a loop type becomes a function call, so no caller branches on loop type). Adapters can declare **launch** — `exec_argv` populates `_ALMANAC_LOOP_ARGV` with the runner's exec tokens (ralph's `mode`→`once.sh`/`afk.sh` under the ralph-loop skill; harden's `almanac harden <target> --loop [--rounds N]`), so the **runner path lives in the adapter, not the launcher**. Adapters also share a **control** contract — the between-round dot-file basename for `stop|steer`. Every loop today uses the same `.${name}-${kind}` convention (`.ralph-stop`/`.ralph-steer`, `.harden-stop`/`.harden-steer`, `.converge-stop`/`.converge-steer`), so the pattern lives once in `lib/loops.sh` as `almanac_loop_default_signal_file` and adapters need not declare `signal_file` at all unless their convention diverges. The public resolver `almanac_loop_signal_file` returns the adapter override if defined else the default — so adding a standard new loop costs zero signal_file code, and three identical case-statements no longer have to stay in sync across adapters. `lib/run.sh` exposes `almanac_loop_run_control_file <type> <base> <kind>` as the absolute-path variant (BASE joined with the resolved basename), so harden-core and converge-core no longer reimplement their own `_control_file` wrapper each. The **launcher** (`lib/loop-launcher.sh`) consumes launch — `_almanac_launch_ralph`/`_almanac_launch_harden` exec their runner via `almanac_loop_adapter_call <type> exec_argv …` rather than a hard-coded path — and the **hub** consumes control through `almanac_loop_run_signal_file` (above). Self-contained like `lib/agent.sh` (no dependency on `lib/core.sh` — only printf/tr/basename/source), it is its own test surface (`test